    <string name="sleep_accessibility_service_description">Tests screen locking on request. The service does not read screen content or record events.</string>
    <string name="sleep_accessibility_settings">Accessibility settings</string>
    <string name="sleep_accessibility_not_connected">Enable the E2Stream service in accessibility settings</string>
    <string name="sleep_accessibility_explain">Enable “E2Stream — sleep test” in accessibility settings, then return here and start the test. After 10 seconds the app will ask the system to lock the screen. This does not guarantee TV standby. Android will show an accessibility permission warning; the service does not read screen content or record events. You can disable it in the same settings after the test.</string>
    <string name="sleep_accessibility_failed">The system rejected the screen lock attempt.</string>
    <string name="sleep_philips_title">Philips network sleep test (beta)</string>
    <string name="sleep_philips_unpaired">Pairing required</string>
    <string name="sleep_philips_pair">Pair</string>
    <string name="sleep_philips_explain">This test uses the local Philips network remote. Pairing requires a phone or computer on the same network. After pairing, start the test; BACK cancels the countdown. Pairing is remembered only until settings are closed. The test does not change timers or require accessibility permission.</string>
    <string name="sleep_philips_browser_instructions">On a phone or computer on the same network, open:\n\n%1$s\n\nOn the page select “Show PIN on TV”, then enter the PIN from the TV screen there. Do not close the PIN window on the TV. The address expires after 3 minutes. The page connection is not encrypted: use only a trusted network and do not share the address.</string>
